{"name":"Halo","hex":"#e2c392","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Halo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the Angels Major League Baseball team.","Alternative letter-case form of HALO."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Halo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Halo (optical phenomenon)","blocks":[{"text":"A halo is an optical phenomenon produced by light interacting with ice crystals suspended in the atmosphere. Halos can have many forms, ranging from colored or white rings to arcs and spots in the sky. Many of these appear near the Sun or Moon, but others occur elsewhere or even in the opposite part of the sky. Among the best known halo types are the circular halo, light pillars, and sun dogs, but many others occur; some are fairly common while others are extremely rare."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Solar_Halo_Feb_13_2021.jpg/330px-Solar_Halo_Feb_13_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halo_(optical_phenomenon)","wikidata":"Q186310"},{"title":"Halo (religious iconography)","blocks":[{"text":"A halo, also called a nimbus, aureole, glory or gloriole, is a crown of light rays, circle or disk of light that surrounds a person in works of art. The halo occurs in the iconography of many religions to indicate holy or sacred figures, and has at various periods also been used in images of rulers and heroes. In the religious art of Ancient Greece, Ancient Rome, Christianity, Hinduism, and Buddhism, sacred people may be depicted with a halo in the form of a circular glow, or flames in Asian art, around the head or around the whole body—this last form is often called a mandorla."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Gandhara_Buddha_%28tnm%29.jpeg/330px-Gandhara_Buddha_%28tnm%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halo_(religious_iconography)","wikidata":"Q3341893"},{"title":"Halo (franchise)","blocks":[{"text":"Halo is an American military science fiction video game series and media franchise, originally developed by Bungie and currently managed and developed by Activision and Halo Studios, part of Microsoft's Xbox Game Studios. The series launched in November 2001 with the first-person shooter video game Halo: Combat Evolved and its tie-in novel, The Fall of Reach. The latest installment, Halo: Campaign Evolved, a remake of the original Combat Evolved campaign newly powered by Unreal Engine 5, was released in July 2026. Spinoffs include real-time strategy and twin-stick shooter games."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halo_(franchise)","wikidata":"Q1747150"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg/500px-Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg/1280px-Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg/500px-Moon_Halo_Over_Mayall_%28Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 22° halo and circumscribed halo around the Moon, observed near Kitt Peak National Observatory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoon_Halo_Over_Mayall_(Moon-Halo-Over-Mayall-3-CC).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg/500px-Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg/1280px-Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg/500px-Ice_crystal_rainbow_%282100189455%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"From top to bottom:A circumzenithal arc, supralateral arc, Parry arc, upper tangent arc, and 22° halo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIce_crystal_rainbow_(2100189455).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/V%C3%A4dersoltavlan_cropped.JPG/330px-V%C3%A4dersoltavlan_cropped.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/V%C3%A4dersoltavlan_cropped.JPG/330px-V%C3%A4dersoltavlan_cropped.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The so-called \"Sun Dog Painting\" (Vädersolstavlan) depicting Stockholm in 1535 and the celestial phenomenon at the time interpreted as an ominous presage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AV%C3%A4dersoltavlan_cropped.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Artificial_CZA.png/500px-Artificial_CZA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Artificial_CZA.png/500px-Artificial_CZA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Analogous refraction demonstration experiment for the circumzenithal arc.[10] Here, it is mistakenly labelled as an artificial rainbow in Gilberts book.[11]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArtificial_CZA.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Artificial_Halo_projected_on_a_spherica